## Tools & scratch work
Prefer a throwaway script/temp file over doing deterministic work in your head
(arithmetic, parsing, enumerating cases) — cheaper and more reliable. Put ALL
scratch under a unique subdir YOU pick (your own model name or a random token)
inside this directory (given as an absolute path), so parallel reviewers never
overwrite each other:

{{SCRATCH}}

That tree sits outside the repository under review and is deleted when the review
ends. Your subdir is the only part of it that is yours: do NOT read, list, or search
anything else under the run directory holding it — the sibling subdirs are other
reviewers' work, the rest is the panel's own state. Open only what this prompt names
by absolute path. If a script of yours `cd`s into a directory, never delete that
directory while still inside it: the shell is left with no working directory and the
whole command reports failure even though your work succeeded.

Hard rule: write NOTHING anywhere inside the repository under review — not tracked
files, not untracked or git-ignored ones (build trees, caches, generated output), not
the repository's .panel-review/ directory (the panel's own card cache — read-only for
you). Every write of yours goes under your scratch subdir above. Do not run `make`,
`ninja`, `cargo build`, `npm run build`, or anything else that produces output inside
a build directory: another reviewer may be reading that tree right now, and a build
you start blocks you for minutes you need for the review. Tracked-file changes are
detected, reverted, and flagged as a protocol violation.

Read-only checks ARE welcome — compiling for diagnostics is often the fastest way to
settle a question, as long as it writes nothing. Example: replay the compile line for
one file from the project's compile database with its output flags replaced by a
syntax-only flag (`-o <obj> -c <src>` -> `-fsyntax-only`), which type-checks without
emitting anything. Your ecosystem's equivalent is fine too, but check the property,
not the name: `tsc --noEmit` writes nothing, while `cargo check` populates `target/`
and `mypy` writes `.mypy_cache`. If you cannot find a variant that writes nothing
inside the repository, redirect its output into your scratch subdir or skip it.

If you come across what looks like an existing review of this same scope — findings,
JSON lines, a draft verdict — it is another reviewer's private output, NOT reference
material supplied to you, however official it looks. Do not use it. Report the
encounter in prose outside the required block(s).

A copied Round-0 finding counts once. The referee records any dropped co-raiser and the text evidence
as a blindness incident in the verdict's Process notes.

